        "verdict": "wrong-severity",
        "severity": "medium",
        "original_severity": "critical",
        "rationale": "Real but OVER-rated, and this is one of the calibration traps the brief names. Confirmed at axon-guest-kernel/src/enforce.rs:295-323: the grant branch prints and calls `clean_halt()` without issuing a syscall; only the deny branch issues one. But the code is now CANDID about exactly why — the doc comment at 290-294 and the inline kprintln both state that the allowed return path needs ring-3 user segments the boot GDT does not yet define and that 'full interpreter ELF load + VFS is the remaining work'. That is declared deferred scope, not a concealed defect. Critically, the security direction is FAIL-CLOSED: a triple fault kills the VM and grants nothing; there is no capability bypass and no attacker gain. The genuine defects are (a) scripts/kernel_enforce_test.sh case 2 greps for the print line and reports '✓ the gate PERMITTED the openat', which asserts something that provably did not happen — a vacuous test carrying a live-enforcement claim, and (b) the harness is referenced by nothing automated. (b) is squarely OSK-P7-H5. What remains uniquely here is a false test assertion plus overclaiming prose. That is a test-integrity/documentation defect: medium.",
