        ],
        "rationale": "Code VERIFIED at crates/axon-vm/src/main.rs:2230-2278 (`measure_and_attest_inner`): AXON_CI_NO_KVM=1 returns Ok early (2243-2246), and an unreadable baseline file falls into the `else` arm that writes the CURRENT digest as the baseline and prints 'attestation: baseline established' (2265-2276). Both halves are as described. Severity lowered on two independent grounds. First, the env-var bypass is NOT a silently-introduced hole: governance/specs/R26-confidential-microvm-substrate.md:5 explicitly documents `AXON_CI_NO_KVM=1` as the blessed CI escape alongside `--no-attest`, and the code prints a visible '[axon-vm] attestation: mock mode' line — it is a spec-sanctioned dev/CI path, so 'silently disabled' overstates it. Second, the TOFU half assumes an attacker who can overwrite the kernel image but is somehow blocked from deleting $HOME/.axon/kernel_baseline.sha256 — both live in the same user trust domain, so local-tamper detection was never going to hold here regardless. What remains genuinely wrong is that the code calls itself a 'mandatory' gate while auto-blessing on first run; that is a real but low-severity trust misstatement with a cheap fix. Effort is medium only because AXON_CI_NO_KVM is referenced by 7+ gate scripts that would each need --no-attest added.",
        "fix_sketch": "In measure_and_attest_inner: delete the AXON_CI_NO_KVM early-return so the ONLY bypass is the explicit --no-attest flag (which already warns), and add --no-attest to the axon-vm invocations in the listed scripts/*.sh. Replace the auto-baseline `else` arm with a refusal ('no pinned baseline at {path}; run `axon-vm attest --pin-baseline` to establish one'), and add an explicit `--pin-baseline` action that performs the write. Keep AXON_CI_NO_KVM for its other, legitimate use (selecting the mock genesis in chain_genesis / skipping the KVM requirement) but stop letting it answer the attestation question.",
