        "effort": "small",
        "files_touched": [
          "crates/axon-os/src/replay.rs",
          "crates/axon-os/src/cli.rs"
        ],
        "rationale": "VERIFIED at crates/axon-os/src/replay.rs:22-40 — `replay` calls `verify(stored)?` (which per record.rs:166-169 only re-walks the record's own event hash chain seeded by the STORED manifest_digest) and then immediately calls `supervisor::run(manifest, supervisor_grant, &stored.run_id, rt)` with the caller-supplied manifest, comparing JSON only afterwards. `stored.manifest_digest` (record.rs:56) is never compared against `canonical_manifest(manifest)` (record.rs:95, already pub) before the re-run. Caller path confirmed at cli.rs:420-464 (cmd_replay loads both from disk and passes them straight in). One refinement to the finding's framing that does NOT change the verdict: a widened grant in a tampered manifest is bounded, because supervisor::run line 28 does `manifest.grant.intersect(supervisor_grant)` — so the real teeth are the `program` field, which is fully attacker-controlled and is what gets executed with the supervisor's authority. That is still an audit command turned into an arbitrary-execution primitive for anyone who can edit the store, which is exactly medium. The check is cheap and clearly intended (the digest is already computed and stored).",
        "fix_sketch": "In replay.rs immediately after `verify(stored)?`, add: `let expect = format!(\"axsha256:{}\", sha256_hex(canonical_manifest(manifest).as_bytes())); if expect != stored.manifest_digest { return Err(VerifyMismatch { detail: \"replay manifest does not match the recorded manifest_digest\".into() }); }` — matching the exact format string used at record.rs:133 so the two cannot drift. Mirror the same check in cmd_replay (cli.rs ~460) before it constructs the runtime, so the CLI never launches on a mismatch. Add a test that a record whose manifest.program has been swapped returns VerifyMismatch with the MockRuntime recording zero runs.",
        "conflicts_with": [],
