      {
        "id": "OSK-M09",
        "verdict": "confirmed",
        "severity": "medium",
        "original_severity": "medium",
        "behavior_change": true,
        "effort": "medium",
        "files_touched": [
          "crates/axon-os/src/runtime.rs",
          "crates/axon-os/src/verdict.rs",
          "crates/axon-core/src/main.rs"
        ],
        "rationale": "VERIFIED at crates/axon-os/src/runtime.rs:376-414. `let err = &proc.stderr;` and the verdict chain is `err.contains(\"sandbox violation\") || err.contains(\"SandboxViolation\") || err.contains(\"not permitted by @[contained]\") || err.contains(\"capability\")` -> Denied{axis:\"sandbox\"}; `err.contains(\"budget\") && (err.contains(\"exhaust\") || err.contains(\"exceeded\"))` -> BudgetExhausted; `err.contains(\"refinement violated\") || err.contains(\"REFINE\")` -> RefineViolation; `err.contains(\"axon: panic\")` -> Denied{axis:\"runtime\"}. Critically, I confirmed there is NO exit-code precondition guarding these branches — the comment at 372-376 explains why (`axon run` propagates main's return value as the exit code, so a program returning 7 is not budget exhaustion), which means the design deliberately relies on a channel the job also writes. So both directions of the finding hold: a job with any IO capability can eprintln(\"budget exceeded\") and have a clean run sealed into the tamper-evident record as BudgetExhausted, and incidental output containing 'capability' flips a clean run to Denied. `first_axon_line` then quotes the job's own text as the containment reason. Medium is right — it corrupts the integrity of the audit record rather than granting capability. NOTE: this shares a root cause with the product-surface MEDIUM 'axon-os maps a job that never ran to Verdict::Completed' (different batch); whichever is scheduled first should carry the fix and the other should be closed as duplicate.",
        "fix_sketch": "Give the interpreter a status channel the job cannot forge. Simplest: `axon run` gains a `--status-fd N` / `--status-file PATH` that the interpreter writes a single JSON line to on exit (`{\"outcome\":\"budget_exhausted\"|\"sandbox_denied\"|\"refine_violation\"|\"panic\"|\"completed\",\"exit\":N}`) from the same carved-exit-code sites that already exist in crates/axon-core/src/main.rs. In runtime.rs, create the status file next to the wrapper, pass the flag, and derive the Verdict from the parsed JSON; treat an absent/unparseable status file as Denied (fail-closed), and demote stderr to advisory `reason` text only. Add a test where the job prints 'budget exceeded' to stderr and still records Completed.",
        "conflicts_with": [],
        "blocks": [],
        "batch": "os-kernel-b"
