      {
        "id": "OSK-M10",
        "verdict": "confirmed",
        "severity": "medium",
        "original_severity": "medium",
        "behavior_change": true,
        "effort": "small",
        "files_touched": [
          "crates/axon-os/src/monitor.rs"
        ],
        "rationale": "VERIFIED at crates/axon-os/src/monitor.rs:129-150. `current_offset += bytes_read;` (line 136) executes BEFORE `std::str::from_utf8(&buf)` (140), and the Err arm sleeps and `continue`s (142-145) — the chunk is dropped and the offset has already advanced past it, so those bytes are never re-read and any violating ledger entries inside them are lost permanently. `line_buf.push_str(chunk)` at 149 with drain only on newline confirms the second half: a line with no '\\n' accumulates without bound in the supervisor process. Medium confirmed on the non-adversarial path alone — any poll that lands mid-multibyte-character (trivially reachable once a reason string contains non-ASCII) silently blinds the R29 compliance monitor for that chunk, and the module's own comment calls this 'skip (I-4 - malformed input)' when the actual effect is data loss, not a skip. Worth flagging for sequencing: the adversarial framing ('a job emits one invalid byte to blank the chunk') is currently blocked by the separate HIGH finding that the contained job cannot write the ledger at all (env_clear drops AXON_AUDIT_LEDGER); it becomes live the moment that HIGH is fixed, so this should land at or before that one.",
        "fix_sketch": "Restructure the read loop to advance only over consumed bytes: replace the `from_utf8` early-continue with `let (good, rest) = match std::str::from_utf8(&buf) { Ok(s) => (s, &[][..]), Err(e) => (unsafe-free: std::str::from_utf8(&buf[..e.valid_up_to()]).unwrap(), &buf[e.valid_up_to()..]) };` then set `current_offset += (bytes_read - rest.len() as u64)` AFTER the split so the incomplete tail is re-read next poll. Distinguish a genuine mid-character split (valid_up_to within the last 3 bytes) from a truly invalid byte, and treat the latter as a violation (deny-by-default, trip the kill file) rather than a skip. Cap line_buf at a fixed size (e.g. 1 MiB) and treat overflow as a violation too. Add tests: a chunk split mid-UTF-8 across two polls still yields the entry; an invalid byte trips the violation flag.",
        "conflicts_with": [],
        "blocks": [],
        "batch": "os-kernel-b"
      },
      {
        "id": "OSK-M11",
        "verdict": "confirmed",
