        "effort": "medium",
        "files_touched": [
          "crates/axon-os/src/cli.rs",
          "crates/axon-os/src/runtime.rs"
        ],
        "rationale": "VERIFIED. crates/axon-os/src/cli.rs uses `std::env::set_var(\"AXON_KILL_FILE\", ...)` at 276 (killable path) and 287 (monitor path), and `std::env::remove_var(\"AXON_KILL_FILE\")` at ~323, ~336 and ~355; crates/axon-os/src/runtime.rs:355 reads it back with `std::env::var_os(\"AXON_KILL_FILE\")` inside run_sandboxed. So process-global env really is the channel between cmd_run and the runtime. The concurrency claim also holds on inspection: the monitor thread is spawned after the set_var at 287 and is only signalled to stop AFTER supervisor::run returns, while the remove_var at ~336 ('clean up the kill file env var after the run') executes while that thread is still alive — a setenv/getenv race with a live thread, which is exactly the pattern Rust 2024 marks `unsafe`. Medium is right: no observed crash today (the monitor thread does not itself read env), but it makes the supervisor non-reentrant (two supervised runs in one process clobber each other's kill file, which would silently disarm a kill switch) and it is a latent edition-migration blocker. behavior_change false — a correct refactor is observationally identical. SCHEDULING: runtime.rs is also touched by OSK-M08 and OSK-M09.",
        "fix_sketch": "Delete the env round-trip. Add a `kill_file: Option<PathBuf>` field to `AxonCoreRuntime` and a constructor `AxonCoreRuntime::from_env().with_kill_file(path)`; in run_sandboxed (runtime.rs:355) read `self.kill_file.as_deref()` instead of `std::env::var_os`. In cli.rs, replace all five set_var/remove_var calls with building the runtime once, after both the killable and monitor branches have decided the path (note the two branches are mutually exclusive today via `killable && monitor_effects.is_none()`, so a single Option suffices). This needs no change to the `Runtime` trait or to supervisor.rs, which keeps the blast radius to two files. Do the same for AXON_BIN/AXON_OS_TIMEOUT_MS only if they are also written by cli.rs — they are read once in from_env, which is fine as-is.",
        "conflicts_with": [],
        "blocks": [],
        "batch": "os-kernel-b"
