        "files_touched": [
          "crates/axon-os/src/grant.rs"
        ],
        "rationale": "VERIFIED at crates/axon-os/src/grant.rs:167-170: `fn is_ancestor(prefix: &str, path: &str) -> bool { path == prefix || path.starts_with(prefix) }` — a raw byte-prefix test with no component boundary, so `is_ancestor(\"./data\", \"./data-secret/\")` is true. Confirmed it is load-bearing in both directions: `prefixes_within` (203-205) builds on it and is used by `is_subset_of` (115-124), the supervisor's delegation-attenuation predicate; and `intersect_prefixes` (211-222) uses it to pick 'the narrower prefix', so intersecting supervisor `./data` with job `./data-secret/` yields `./data-secret/` — a region the supervisor never authorized. `host_matches` (176-181) has the identical shape: `pat.strip_prefix('*')` then `host.ends_with(suffix)`, so pattern `*x.com` matches `evilx.com` (the doc comment only contemplates `*.x.com`). All three are real unsoundness in the capability-attenuation core. Held at medium rather than raised to high ONLY because a separate CRITICAL finding establishes that these path/host allowlists are never enforced at runtime at all (the runtime sandbox is coarse IO/Net/AI tags), so today the confusion has no filesystem consequence. That makes sequencing important: this must land at or before the runtime-enforcement fix, or fixing enforcement immediately yields a live escape. Not reproduced by execution; the three functions are pure and unambiguous.",
        "fix_sketch": "In grant.rs, make is_ancestor boundary-aware: normalize by ensuring the prefix ends in '/' (`let p = if prefix.ends_with('/') { prefix.to_string() } else { format!(\"{prefix}/\") }`) and return `path == prefix || path == p.trim_end_matches('/') || path.starts_with(&p)`. Restrict host_matches to a leading-label glob: accept only patterns starting with `*.` and match `host == &pat[2..] || host.ends_with(pat.strip_prefix('*').unwrap())` where the stripped suffix begins with '.'; reject/deny any other `*` placement rather than treating it as a bare suffix match. Add the two negative tests the finding names — `./data` must NOT contain `./data-secret/`, and `*x.com` must NOT match `evilx.com` — plus a positive `./data` contains `./data/sub` and `*.x.com` matches `a.x.com`.",
