        "rationale": "VERIFIED at crates/axon-guest-kernel/src/bump.rs:29-44. `let aligned = (current + align - 1) & !(align - 1); let next = aligned + size; if next > HEAP_END { panic!(...) }` — both additions are unchecked, and since this is a no_std release kernel build (no debug assertions) they wrap silently, so a `size` near usize::MAX produces a small `next` that passes the bound check and `alloc` returns `aligned` into the middle of the kernel image; alloc_zeroed (46-50) then write_bytes over it. Note `debug_assert!(align.is_power_of_two())` at 31 is likewise compiled out in release. The finding's own reachability caveat is accurate and is why low is correct — every current caller passes kernel-internal sizes, so there is no path from untrusted input to this today. It is worth fixing anyway because it is a two-line change and this is the allocator that will sit underneath the future interpreter load. behavior_change false: no reachable input changes behavior; the panic message is the only observable difference and it already exists for the exhaustion case.",
        "fix_sketch": "Rewrite the loop body as: `let aligned = match current.checked_next_multiple_of(align) { Some(a) => a, None => panic!(\"kernel heap: alignment overflow\") }; let next = match aligned.checked_add(size) { Some(n) if n <= HEAP_END => n, _ => panic!(\"kernel heap exhausted\") };` leaving the compare_exchange retry loop unchanged. Promote the `debug_assert!(align.is_power_of_two())` to a real `assert!` so it survives release builds, since a non-power-of-two align silently corrupts the mask.",
