      {
        "id": "INTERP-C01",
        "verdict": "confirmed",
        "severity": "critical",
        "original_severity": "critical",
        "rationale": "READ + REPRODUCED. Code is unchanged from the report: interp/builtins.rs:191 is literally `if sb_handle >= 0 && name != \"sandbox_create\" && name != \"sandbox_run\"`, and interp/builtins.rs:2836 is `self.active_sandbox.replace(sb_handle)` (a Cell<i64> single slot, interp.rs:477 — not a stack, so save/restore gives nesting-safety of the HANDLE but zero monotonicity of the CEILING). sandbox_create (:2798) does no intersection with the active ceiling at all. Repro: a program whose main does `sandbox_create(0,\"\")` then `sandbox_run(sb,\"evil\",42)`, where `evil` does `sandbox_create(0,\"IO,Net,AI,Exec,FS\")` + `sandbox_run(wide,\"inner\",x)`, printed from inside the nominally-pure ceiling and exited 0. Severity is NOT inflated by the repo's usual 'security-named module = critical' bias: the attacker here is exactly the population F5 exists to contain (untrusted/AI-emitted code passed to sandbox_run by name), no pre-existing trusted foothold is required, and axon-os wraps every job in this mechanism, so it is the runtime backstop for the whole grant story. CRITICAL stands.",
        "files_touched": [
