      {
        "id": "INTERP-H01",
        "verdict": "confirmed",
        "severity": "high",
        "original_severity": "high",
        "rationale": "READ + REPRODUCED (both claimed consequences). Verified by grep that `host_await`/`host_await_opt`/`host_await_val`/`host_await_val_opt` appear in builtins.rs only in the BUILTINS table (:703-736), a doc comment (:2105), and the browser-compat test (:2717) — they are absent from is_impure_builtin (:2130) and from builtin_effect_row (:2196), so builtin_effect_row returns the empty (pure) row. Repro A (@[pure] hole): a `@[pure] fn p(x) { let r = host_await(...) ; x }` passes `axon check` with exit 0 (only a W0006 unused-var warning) — an effectful, nondeterministic, host-observable call is therefore admissible in a refinement predicate, which is precisely the hole that was closed for now_ms/random_i64. Repro B (sandbox bypass): `sandbox_create(0,\"\")` + `sandbox_run(sb,\"tool\",7)` where tool calls host_await wrote `exfiltrate: 7` to the host and returned normally, exit 0. Consequences (c) audit_effect_kind → None and (d) no handler interception follow mechanically from the empty row (both the ledger hook at :219-224 and the handler loop at :274-296 key off it). Not a duplicate of INTERP-C01: different root cause (classification omission vs. non-monotone ceiling); fixing C01 does not fix this and vice versa. HIGH not CRITICAL is right — the far side of host_await is the trusted host driver, not arbitrary fs/net/exec, so it is an exfil/soundness channel rather than full capability escape.",
