      {
        "id": "F145",
        "verdict": "needs-human",
        "original_severity": "medium",
        "severity": "medium",
        "needs_human_reason": "The fix is a language-semantics change with no non-breaking option. Reserving 3..=12 from main's return value removes a capability every existing program has (returning an arbitrary i64 exit status), and any static W/E at check time will fire on real programs. The additive alternative — a separate structured status channel supervisors read instead of the exit code — requires axon-os, axon-vm and the deploy pipeline to change how they read outcomes, and leaves the documented exit-code contract knowingly ambiguous in the meantime. Which of {clamp/refuse, transform main's value, add a status channel, downgrade the six doc comments to describe reality} is shipped is a product call on the supervision contract, not an engineering one.",
        "rationale": "Reproduced exactly on the interpreter: main()=3 -> exit 3 (collides with VERIFY_FAILED), =6 -> 6 (REFINE_VIOLATION), =8 -> 8 (SANDBOX_VIOLATION), =256 -> exit 0 (a program returning 256 reports SUCCESS), =300 -> 44. The doc comments the finding cites are verbatim in interp.rs:238-248 and do claim 'a supervisor can branch on X specifically', which is false for any supervisor that also sees main's return. I considered raising this to high — it is the root cause the axon-os findings point at, and runtime.rs's stderr-sniffing (itself the subject of two higher-severity findings) exists precisely because of it — but the severity is not the blocker here; the absence of a non-breaking fix is, so it routes to a human rather than into the build queue at any severity.",
        "files_touched": [
          "crates/axon-core/src/interp.rs",
          "crates/axon-core/src/main.rs",
          "crates/axon-core/src/checker.rs",
          "crates/axon-os/src/runtime.rs"
        ],
        "effort": "medium",
        "behavior_change": true,
        "fix_sketch": "Ship the no-decision part now regardless: correct the six exit-code doc comments in interp.rs:~220-248 so they state the collision with main's return instead of promising a guarantee that does not hold. Then, per the human's choice: (i) clamp/refuse — emit a W/E from checker.rs when `main`'s return type is i64 and any statically-known returned constant falls in 3..=12, and have main.rs saturate rather than truncate values >255 (currently `300 -> 44`); or (ii) status channel — have the interpreter/native runtime emit a structured terminal-status record and change crates/axon-os/src/runtime.rs:373-376 to read it instead of substring-matching stderr, which retires the sniffing findings too.",
        "conflicts_with": [
          "F150"
        ],
        "blocks": [],
        "batch": "interp-b"
      },
      {
        "id": "F180",
        "verdict": "confirmed",
        "original_severity": "medium",
        "severity": "medium",
        "rationale": "Code confirmed verbatim at crates/axon-core/src/interp.rs:288-296: `assign` does `scope.contains_key(name)` and then `scope.insert(name.to_string(), val)` — a second hash plus a fresh String allocation to overwrite a key that is already there and already identical. `get` (:285-287) is the described `iter().rev().find_map(|s| s.get(name))`, i.e. one SipHash per scope level. I did not re-run the malloc counter, so the specific 1.00-malloc/iteration and ns/iter figures are unverified by me; the allocation itself is unambiguous from the source and needs no measurement. Medium is right and consistent with how the other measured interpreter perf findings in this batch are rated. IMPORTANT SCOPING: the finding bundles a two-line fix with a whole-resolver redesign — schedule only the `assign` fix under this id; the (depth, slot) resolution redesign is a separate large task that will collide with F177 and F183.",
        "files_touched": [
          "crates/axon-core/src/interp.rs"
        ],
        "effort": "trivial",
        "behavior_change": false,
        "fix_sketch": "Replace the body of `Env::assign` (interp.rs:288-296) with `for scope in self.scopes.iter_mut().rev() { if let Some(slot) = scope.get_mut(name) { *slot = val; return true; } } false` — one lookup, zero allocations. Note the existing `get_mut` (:298-300) already does exactly this traversal, so `assign` can simply be `match self.get_mut(name) { Some(slot) => { *slot = val; true } None => false }`. Do NOT attempt the (depth, slot) resolution redesign in this task.",
