      {
        "id": "RT-01",
        "verdict": "confirmed",
        "severity": "high",
        "original_severity": "high",
        "rationale": "Verified in source. `complete_structured_inner` (crates/axon-ai/src/lib.rs:566-621) goes straight to `api_key(p)?` and a live `reqwest::blocking` POST with no `ai_mock_enabled()` check anywhere in it or in its callers `complete_typed_uncertain_inner`/`complete_typed_flat_inner`; only `ai_complete_inner_model_usage` (line 309) honors the mock. All five `__axon_ai_extract_*` externs (775/825/883/927/976) route through it. The interpreter short-circuits under mock. So `AXON_AI_MOCK=1 ./native_binary` calling ai_extract_* diverges from `axon run` and attempts a live billed HTTPS call (or errors on a missing key). This is the I-2 divergence class the project gates on, and it contradicts the recorded 'codegen AI-mock gap' resolution which only covered ai_complete. Not reproduced end-to-end (would require a native build of an ai_extract example plus a live key to see the billing half) — confidence rests on code reading, which is unambiguous here.",
        "files_touched": [
          "crates/axon-ai/src/lib.rs",
          "crates/axon-core/src/interp/builtins.rs",
          "scripts/fuzz_parity.sh"
        ],
        "effort": "small",
        "fix_sketch": "At the top of `complete_structured_inner` (lib.rs:566), before `api_key(p)?`, add `if ai_mock_enabled() { return Ok(serde_json::json!({\"value\": <mock for value_type>, \"confidence\": 0.9})) }`, with the mock value matched byte-for-byte to the interpreter's stubs at interp/builtins.rs:~4364 (i64 -> 1, f64 -> 1.0, bool -> true, confidence 0.9). Add a parity-harness row exercising each ai_extract_* builtin under AXON_AI_MOCK=1 native vs interp. Fail-first test: assert the native binary produces the interpreter's output with no API key present.",
        "behavior_change": true,
        "blocks": [],
        "conflicts_with": [],
        "batch": "docs-runtime"
      },
      {
        "id": "RT-02",
        "verdict": "confirmed",
        "severity": "high",
        "original_severity": "high",
        "rationale": "Both halves verified in crates/axon-ai/src/lib.rs. (1) `find_dotenv_upwards` (line 80) walks from current_dir to `/` looking for `.env` and `apply_dotenv` sets the parsed KEY=VALUEs into the process env; `load_dotenv_once` is called from `provider()` and `base_url()`, i.e. on the first AI call, inside the runtime TCB with no capability check. (2) `base_url` (line 150) resolves AXON_AI_BASE_URL, then ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL, then the default — while the static check pins the implicit ai_complete host to the constant api.anthropic.com. So the host the capability checker validates and the host the runtime dials are independent, and a program with any fs:write grant can bridge them via `./.env`. Prompt plus the real x-api-key go to the attacker host while the static check passes. That is a capability-boundary bypass in the project's headline guarantee, not a hypothetical — HIGH confirmed. Partial mitigation already present: AXON_DOTENV exists as an explicit-path override, so recommendation (a) is half-built; the upward walk is still the default. Not reproduced live (I did not want to make a network call); code path is unambiguous.",
        "files_touched": [
          "crates/axon-ai/src/lib.rs",
          "crates/axon-core/src/capabilities.rs",
          "crates/axon-core/src/interp/builtins.rs"
        ],
        "effort": "medium",
        "fix_sketch": "(a) In load_dotenv_once (lib.rs:65-77), drop find_dotenv_upwards from the default path: load only from AXON_DOTENV, or from the invocation directory behind an explicit opt-in env/flag; never walk to filesystem root. (b) Close the pin gap: have the interpreter/runtime pass the effective net allowlist (or a 'contained' flag) into axon-ai, and in endpoint_url/base_url validate the RESOLVED host against that allowlist — refuse AXON_AI_BASE_URL/ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL overrides entirely when the program was checked with @[contained], so capabilities.rs:90 ai_builtin_host and the wire behavior agree. Fail-first test: an @[contained(fs:[write(\"./\")], net:[\"api.anthropic.com\")]) program that writes ./.env with AXON_AI_BASE_URL must be refused at the ai_complete call.",
        "behavior_change": true,
        "blocks": [],
        "conflicts_with": [],
        "batch": "docs-runtime"
      },
      {
        "id": "RT-03",
        "verdict": "confirmed",
        "severity": "medium",
        "original_severity": "medium",
        "rationale": "Verified. dict_abort_if_nonint is defined at crates/axon-rt/src/lib.rs:255 and called only from __axon_dict_values (:708) and __axon_dict_to_pairs (:842). __axon_dict_map_values (:911) builds its snapshot with `DictVal::Float(fl) => fl.to_bits() as i64, DictVal::Str(s) => s.as_ptr() as i64` and hands those raw i64s to the user closure — a heap address masquerading as data, the exact I-9 case the guard exists to prevent; __axon_dict_filter (:1005) and __axon_dict_each (:1049) are the same shape. One correction to the finding: __axon_dict_to_str (:953) does NOT leak pointer bits — it renders `DictVal::Str(s) => s.clone()` — but its `DictVal::Float(f) => format!(\"{f}\")` is a real textual divergence from the interpreter's canonical %.6g, so the finding's conclusion for that function survives on different grounds. Medium is right: silent wrong answers, but only on a v1-unsupported dict shape reached through native codegen.",
        "files_touched": [
          "crates/axon-rt/src/lib.rs",
          "scripts/fuzz_parity.sh"
        ],
        "effort": "small",
        "fix_sketch": "Call `dict_abort_if_nonint(&guard, \"dict_map_values\"|\"dict_filter\"|\"dict_each\")` at the top of lib.rs:911, :1005 and :1049 (take the lock, check, then proceed) so all four bulk paths share the loud-abort policy. For __axon_dict_to_str (:953), either add the same guard or fix the Float arm to use the canonical %.6g formatter (axon_fmt_g) instead of `format!(\"{f}\")` — the guard is the simpler v1-consistent choice. Add fuzz-parity rows building a str-valued and a float-valued dict and hitting each of the four bulk APIs, asserting native and interp agree (including on the abort exit code).",
        "behavior_change": true,
        "blocks": [],
        "conflicts_with": [],
        "batch": "docs-runtime"
      },
      {
        "id": "RT-04",
        "verdict": "confirmed",
        "severity": "medium",
        "original_severity": "medium",
        "rationale": "Verified by grep: AXON_AUDIT_LEDGER appears only in crates/axon-core/src/main.rs (:671-675) and crates/axon-core/src/interp/builtins.rs (:212-219, :4247-4249) — zero hits in crates/axon-rt, crates/axon-ai or crates/axon-core/src/codegen. Same for AXON_AI_REPLAY. So a native `axon build` binary silently ignores both: no hash-chained R28 ledger entries and no F2 replay memoization, meaning the 'exactly reproducible AI run' guarantee holds only under the interpreter. The second store (provenance.jsonl via __axon_log_agent_action) is real and separate. Medium is right — this is a scope/architecture gap between two engines rather than a bypass of an enforced check, but 'silently ignored' is the bad failure mode and is what earns it medium rather than low.",
        "files_touched": [
          "crates/axon-rt/src/lib.rs",
          "crates/axon-rt/src/provenance.rs",
          "crates/axon-rt/Cargo.toml",
          "crates/axon-ai/src/lib.rs",
          "crates/axon-core/src/main.rs"
        ],
        "effort": "large",
        "fix_sketch": "Two-stage. Stage 1 (small, do first): in main.rs's build path, if AXON_AUDIT_LEDGER or AXON_AI_REPLAY is set, emit a loud warning (or refuse) that these are interpreter-only — makes the gap visible instead of silent. Stage 2 (large): add an axon-audit dependency to axon-rt, honor AXON_AUDIT_LEDGER in the runtime's capability-bearing externs (ai/fs/net/exec) via append_global, and make __axon_log_agent_action fail-closed or warn-once instead of swallowing create_dir_all/write failures with `let _ =` (provenance.rs:~353). Depends on RT-05 giving append_global a distinguishable not-configured result, otherwise fail-closed cannot be implemented correctly.",
        "behavior_change": true,
        "blocks": [],
        "conflicts_with": [],
        "batch": "docs-runtime"
      },
      {
        "id": "RT-05",
        "verdict": "confirmed",
        "severity": "medium",
        "original_severity": "medium",
        "rationale": "Both claims verified in crates/axon-audit/src/lib.rs. verify_chain (:349) iterates the entries it is given, checking seq == i, prev_hash linkage and a recomputed entry_hash — every one of those still holds after deleting the last k lines of the JSONL, so tail truncation is undetectable; only interior deletion/modification is caught (which is what the existing test covers). set_ledger_path (:399) swallows an open failure with a single eprintln and leaves the ledger None, and append_global (:409) then returns `Ok(0)` for every call with the comment 'no ledger configured — fail-open', so callers cannot distinguish 'recorded at seq 0' from 'not recorded at all'. I deliberately did NOT inflate this to high despite it being in a security-named crate (documented bias b): the fix requires an external commitment, which is genuinely outside what an append-only local file can provide, and the affected claim is in a crate doc comment rather than an enforced check.",
        "files_touched": [
          "crates/axon-audit/src/lib.rs",
          "crates/axon-core/src/main.rs"
        ],
        "effort": "medium",
        "fix_sketch": "(1) Truncation: make append_global/flush_ledger maintain an external head commitment — write the latest (seq, entry_hash) to a sidecar file (and/or stderr at flush) and have verify_chain take an expected head so a short-but-valid chain fails verification. (2) Fail-open: change append_global's None arm from Ok(0) to a distinguishable `Err(NotConfigured)` (or an AppendOutcome enum) so callers can tell the two cases apart, and have set_ledger_path propagate the open error to main.rs:671-675 which should abort the run when AXON_AUDIT_LEDGER was explicitly set — matching the project's fail-closed posture. Add a regression test that truncates the last entry and asserts verification fails.",
        "behavior_change": true,
